  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...A plume of tropical moisture characterized by \n2\"+ PWATs and dewpoints in the 70s is forecast to get pulled \nnorth through the southeastern CONUS over the weekend to the \neast of the northward-advancing remnants of TC Isaias. At the \nsurface, a warm front/coastal trough will trudge north along \nthe southeastern coastline, likely with a weak, non-tropical \narea of low pressure developing along it. Along and ahead of the\nwarm front, a strong southeasterly LLJ will develop, supporting\nincreasing WAA and moisture transport into the area with a \nshield of moderate to heavy rain developing across the coastal \nCarolinas. Along and just behind the warm front, the precip \nwill likely become more banded and contain some embedded \nconvective elements with very heavy rain. It's these bands that \nwill pose the greatest risk of flooding. The time period of most\nconcern for these heavy rates and flooding potential are \novernight Sat night into early Sun morning. Convective guidance \nsuggests rainfall amounts as high as 4-6\"+ are possible if those\nbands manage to reach the coast. As is often the case with \nconvection nearing the coast, whether some of the moisture \ntransport will be cutoff by strong convection over the Gulf \nStream is in question. If this happens, amounts would be quite \na bit less for areas outside of the coastal waters. 09/12Z REFS\nLPPM has come up with amounts however, suggesting amounts in \nthe 4-6+\" range possible for the immediate coast and OBX zones.\nFor this reason, have gone ahead and issued a Flood Watch for \nall but the interior ENC zones effective later Sat afternoon \nthrough early Sun afternoon.\n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Possibility of convective elements reaching the\ncoast would warrant a tornado risk later Sat evening as the \naforementioned warm front lifts through ENC. Plenty of low level\nhelicity in place with the inc serly flow in place and strong\nveering wind profile downstairs. The greatest tor threat would\nlikely be focused near the srn/sern coast where better\ninstability advects onshore. For Sunday, flow becomes more\nunidirectional with swrly low level winds ensuing during the\nafternoon. While helicity wanes, better instability will be in \nplace due to daytime highs expected in the low 80s and TD's in \nthe 70s. Will have to watch for continued severe threat Sunday \nafternoon ahead of the cold front, esp if there are some breaks \nin the cloud cover. \n\nThe remnants of Isaias are forecast to round the upper level \nridge and move off the Northern Mid-Atlantic Coast early next \nweek. Drier air then moves in behind it with drying conditions\ndeveloping Sun night into Mon.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 3...Strengthening onshore winds, building seas, and\nhigher than normal astronomical tides may support some coastal \nimpacts this weekend. There remains uncertainty on the magnitude\nof impacts, especially given the shorter-duration of the \nstronger winds, as well as what should be a weaker swell \ncompared to the recent Nor'easter. With that in mind, there will\nbe an increased risk of rip currents and large waves, at a \nminimum. High Surf Advisory remains in effect between Ocracoke \nand Oregon Inlets focused on Sunday. Additionally, some of the \nmost vulnerable locations along the Outer Banks may see at least\nsome minor issues, especially with Sunday morning's high tide \n(7-9am). There may also be some minor coastal flooding around \nsounds and tidal waterways around the Crystal Coast due to the \nhigher astronomical tides.",
